@Introduction
CONXLIB is a single-file C console user interface
library for DOS, Windows and OS/2 text consoles. It
brings a classic Borland / Turbo Vision look to plain
conio programs: framed windows, pull-down menus, menu
bars, list boxes, buttons, check boxes, radio option
and property lists, a grid picker, a single and a
multiple file selector, a scrollable text viewer, a
calendar, message and input boxes, mouse and wheel
support, a status line and a built-in help engine with
cross-topic hyperlinks.

The whole library is one conxlib.c plus conxlib.h, so
you drop it into a project and link it; there is no
extra object file or resource to manage. It compiles
clean under Open Watcom, Borland C++ 3.1, Turbo C++
2.0 and Microsoft C, in 16-bit real-mode DOS, 32-bit
flat DOS (DOS4GW) and Win32 console builds, all from
the same source with a few ifdefs.

A typical program starts with @hello, builds the
screen with @box and @blocks, runs a @menubar or
a @menu, reads input with @getkey and @pollmouse,
then cleans up with @bye. For a map of every public
function follow @Index; to compile and link the
library follow @Build. The color and attribute model
is in @Colors and @Attributes, the key codes in @Keys.
@Index
Functions grouped by area. Follow a link to read its
topic.

Startup:  @hello @bye @exittodos @shell @about
Screen:   @box @cputsc @blocks @clock @status
Input:    @getkey @kbhit @pollmouse @getstr @getpw
Menus:    @menu @menubar @buttons @option @property
Pickers:  @listbox @grid @fileselector @filemultisel
Dialogs:  @msgbox @inputbox @inputmask @error
Display:  @view @help @helpindex @tooltip
Files:    @filesize @getdiskfree @getcountry
Themes:   @theme @gettheme @theme_btnattrs
Misc:     @savescr @restorescr @progressbar
          @screensaver @calendar @compilerstring
Concepts: @Colors @Attributes @Keys @Build @License

The shared mouse state is @mousestatus. To open help
from any widget press F1; inside help, TAB
cycles the @help hyperlinks and ENTER follows one.
@Colors
Color constants for CONIOXLIB_ATTR; the macro is
in @Attributes. They match the BIOS / Win32 console
console palette:

  BLACK        0   BLUE         1
  GREEN        2   CYAN         3
  RED          4   MAGENTA      5
  BROWN        6   LIGHTGRAY    7
  DARKGRAY     8   LIGHTBLUE    9
  LIGHTGREEN  10   LIGHTCYAN   11
  LIGHTRED    12   LIGHTMAGENTA 13
  YELLOW      14   WHITE       15

The high bit of the background (bit 7) is the blink
flag on real DOS, so backgrounds 8..15 blink unless
vgaremap or the BIOS disabled blink. Foregrounds 8..15
are the bright variants of 0..7 and never blink.
Themes (see @theme) own their full attribute triple,
so a dialog inherits the active theme colors.
@Attributes
An attribute byte is (background << 4) | foreground.
Build it with the macro:

  CONIOXLIB_ATTR(fg, bg)
    -> ((bg) << 4) | (fg)

Example: CONIOXLIB_ATTR(WHITE, BLUE) is white text on
a blue background, the value 0x1F. Pass attributes to
every widget as atr0 (frame / unselected) and atr1
(selected / hot). @Colors lists the named constants.
The active theme (see @theme) stores the recommended
attribute for each role; conioxlib_theme_btnattrs()
returns the button triple of the active theme so a
dialog matches the rest of the UI.
@Keys
Portable key codes returned by conioxlib_getkey() and
_conioxlib_pollkey(). Extended keys arrive as
(scan << 8), so KBD_UP, KBD_DOWN ... KBD_F1 share the
high byte. ASCII keys arrive as their code:

  KBD_ENTER  0x0D   KBD_ESC  KEY_ESC
  KBD_TAB    0x09   KBD_F1   (KEY_F1 << 8)
  KBD_UP    (KEY_UP << 8)    KBD_DOWN  (KEY_DOWN<<8)
  KBD_LEFT  (KEY_LEFT << 8)  KBD_RIGHT (KEY_RIGHT<<8)
  KBD_PGUP  (KEY_PGUP << 8)  KBD_PGDW  (KEY_PGDN<<8)
  KBD_ORG   (KEY_HOME << 8)  KBD_END   (KEY_END<<8)

Test a key with == KBD_UP etc. The mouse wheel is
mapped to KBD_UP / KBD_DOWN by _conioxlib_mouse_nav
when the caller does not claim the event; a right
click maps to KBD_F1 so right-click opens @help.
@Build
Compile conxlib.c with your compiler and link it into
the program; no extra object or resource is needed.

Open Watcom (16-bit DOS, large model):
  wcc -ml -3 -d2 conxlib.c
  wcc -ml -3 -d2 conxdemo.c
  wlink system dos name conxdemo file conxlib,conxdemo

Open Watcom (32-bit flat DOS, DOS4GW):
  wcc386 -3r -d2 conxlib.c   (and conxdemo.c)
  wlink system pmodew name conxdemo file conxlib,\
    conxdemo

Borland / Turbo C++ (16-bit DOS, large model):
  bcc -ml -d conxlib.c conxdemo.c

Win32 console (Open Watcom):
  wcc386 -d_WIN32 conxlib.c   (and conxdemo.c)
  wlink system nt name conxdemo file conxlib,conxdemo

conxlib auto-detects the platform from the compiler
predefined macros (__WATCOMC__, __BORLANDC__,
__TURBOC__, _WIN32). See @Introduction for an overview
and @License for redistribution terms.
