The home page is https://nikkhokkho.sourceforge.io
See @Introduction for what the library is and @Build
for how to compile it.
@Main
Welcome to the CONXLIB 2.0 demonstration program.
Pick an option with the arrow keys, hotkeys or the mouse.
Press F1 for help. ESC in the main menu acts as Exit.
This help file documents every public function and
variable of conxlib: one topic per symbol, each with a
prototype, a short description, the parameters, the
return value and a ready-to-run example. It is also a
stress data set for the help engine: 58 topics, enough
to exercise the malloc'd topic cache, the offset seek
and the 60/15 column truncation.
See also: @Introduction @Index
@Mouse
conioxlib_pollmouse() updates the global
conioxlib_mousestatus (see @mousestatus) with x, y,
lclick, rclick, wheelx and wheely. Call it once per
frame, before testing any control. On Windows the
cursor position is polled every frame; on DOS the
library pointer is drawn by conioxlib_pollmouse() when
vgaremap(1) has hidden the driver pointer.
@Exit
Exit asks for confirmation through conioxlib_exittodos().
No keeps running the demo, Yes ends the program and
Shell spawns a DOS shell and comes back afterwards.
conioxlib_bye() restores the screen saved by
conioxlib_hello() and frees the help topic cache before
the process ends.
@mousestatus
Prototype:
extern struct conioxlib_smousestatus
    conioxlib_mousestatus;
The shared mouse state read by every widget.
Fields: x and y are the screen cell under the pointer
(1-based); wheelx and wheely are deltas since the last
poll; lclick and rclick are edge flags (1 = single
click, 2 = double click) that stay set until the next
conioxlib_pollmouse(). Read it after polling; never
write to it.
Example:
  conioxlib_pollmouse();
  if (conioxlib_mousestatus.lclick)
  {
      box(conioxlib_mousestatus.x,
          conioxlib_mousestatus.y, 40, 12,
          atr, A_DOUBLE, "Click", SHADOW);
  }
See also: @pollmouse @Mouse
@getkey
Prototype:
unsigned int conioxlib_getkey(void);
Reads the next key from the keyboard.
Parameters: none.
Plain keys arrive as their ASCII code and extended
keys as (scan code << 8), so the KBD_ constants can be
compared directly. It also drains the pending keys
captured by conioxlib_pollmouse() under Windows.
Returns: the key code (0 only if the queue is empty
and no raw key is waiting, which should not happen
after conioxlib_kbhit() returns nonzero).
Example:
  if (conioxlib_kbhit())
  {
      k = conioxlib_getkey();
      if (k == KBD_ESC) { /* cancel */ }
  }
See also: @kbhit @pollmouse @Keys
@kbhit
Prototype:
int conioxlib_kbhit(void);
Returns nonzero when a key is waiting.
Parameters: none.
It sees both the keyboard buffer and the pending keys
kept by conioxlib_pollmouse() under Windows, so it is
the safe replacement for the raw kbhit().
Returns: nonzero when a key is available, 0 otherwise.
Example:
  while (!conioxlib_kbhit())
  {
      conioxlib_clock();
  }
@pollmouse
Prototype:
void conioxlib_pollmouse(void);
Updates conioxlib_mousestatus with the mouse state.
Parameters: none.
Call it once per frame, before testing any control.
The wheel fields are deltas since the last call; the
click fields are edges cleared at the top of the next
poll. Under DOS with vgaremap(1) it also draws the
library mouse pointer.
Example:
  conioxlib_pollmouse();
  if (conioxlib_mousestatus.lclick)
  {
      /* handle the click */
  }
See also: @mousestatus @Mouse
@box
Prototype:
void conioxlib_box(unsigned short x1,
    unsigned short y1, unsigned short x2,
    unsigned short y2, unsigned int atr,
    const char *frame, const char *title,
    unsigned int flags);
Draws a framed window from (x1,y1) to (x2,y2).
Parameters: atr is the frame color; frame is one of
A_NONE, A_BLOCK, A_SINGLE, A_DOUBLE, S_SINGLE,
S_DOUBLE, U_SINGLE or U_DOUBLE; title is painted
centered on the top border; flags combines SHADOW,
CLOSE, NOWINDOW and ZOOM.
ZOOM animates the frame in 10 steps from the center,
or from the last menu()/menubar() pick when one is
pending (the "open from the menu bar" combo); any
key aborts the animation. No effect on the final
painted window.
Example:
  box(3, 3, 36, 23, CONIOXLIB_ATTR(BLACK, CYAN),
      A_DOUBLE, "Files",
      SHADOW | CLOSE | ZOOM);
See also: @cputsc @blocks @savescr @Colors
@cputsc
Prototype:
void conioxlib_cputsc(unsigned short x1,
    unsigned short x2, unsigned short y,
    const char *str);
Writes str centered between columns x1 and x2 on row y.
Parameters: x1 and x2 define the centering range, y is
the destination row and str the text to write.
Example:
  cputsc(20, 60, 9, "Title");
@blocks
Prototype:
void conioxlib_blocks(void);
Draws the lock indicator panel of line 25.
Parameters: none.
It paints the INS, CAP, NUM and SCR mnemonics in the
locks strip, upper case when the lock is active. A
packed state word is compared with the previous one
and nothing is repainted unless it changed or line 25
was wiped. Also refreshed by conioxlib_clock().
Example:
  conioxlib_blocks();
@clock
Prototype:
void conioxlib_clock(void);
Refreshes the clock of line 25.
Parameters: none.
It shows the time in the clock strip with the color of
the active theme and also refreshes the lock
indicators. Call it inside every wait loop; the
compatibility alias is conioxlib_watch().
Example:
  while (!conioxlib_kbhit())
  {
      conioxlib_watch();
  }
@getcountry
Prototype:
void conioxlib_getcountry(
    struct conioxlib_scountry *c);
Fills c with the DOS/Windows country information.
Parameters: c receives the date format, the currency
symbol, the thousand, decimal, date and time
separators, the currency style and digits and the time
format.
Example:
  struct conioxlib_scountry c;
  conioxlib_getcountry(&c);
  printf("Currency: %s", c.curr);
@getdiskfree
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_getdiskfree_ext(
    unsigned char drive,
    struct conioxlib_sdiskfree *df);
Fills df with the free and total space of a drive.
Parameters: drive is 0 for the current drive, 1 for A,
2 for B and so on; df receives the sectors per
cluster, the bytes per sector and the free and total
clusters.
Returns: 0 on success and 1 on error. DOS uses the
FAT32 aware int 21h AX=7303h with the AH=36h fallback.
Example:
  struct conioxlib_sdiskfree df;
  if (conioxlib_getdiskfree_ext(3, &df) == 0)
  {
      printf("%lu KB free",
          (df.avail_clusters *
           df.sectors_per_cluster *
           df.bytes_per_sector) / 1024UL);
  }
@filesize
Prototype:
unsigned long int conioxlib_filesize(
    const char *path);
Returns the length of a file in bytes.
Parameters: path is the file to measure.
Returns: the byte length, or 0xFFFFFFFFUL when the
file cannot be opened or read.
Example:
  fsz = conioxlib_filesize("CONXDEMO.C");
@setpal
Prototype:
void conioxlib_setpal(unsigned char e);
Changes the text mode DAC palette.
Parameters: e selects the palette: 0 restores the
classic 16 color palette and any other value applies
the library palette used by the demo.
It only works under DOS; on Windows it does nothing
because the console has no DAC registers.
Example:
  conioxlib_setpal(1);
@vgaremap
Prototype:
void conioxlib_vgaremap(unsigned char on);
Remaps glyphs of the 8x16 VGA font (DOS only).
Parameters: on enables the library mouse pointer when
nonzero and restores the driver pointer when zero.
With 1 it reprograms glyphs 0x01 (mouse pointer),
0x10 and 0x11 (solid triangles) and 0x18 and 0x19
(chunky arrows) so the scroll bars and the pointer
look like Turbo Vision. It is a no-op on Windows and
32-bit Watcom.
Example:
  conioxlib_vgaremap(1);
@push
Prototype:
void conioxlib_push(void);
Clears the screen with a push effect.
Parameters: none.
The screen is folded away zone by zone in six passes
and any key aborts the effect.
Example:
  conioxlib_push();
@bounce
Prototype:
void conioxlib_bounce(void);
Clears the screen with a bounce effect.
Parameters: none.
The screen is thrown down and bounces back up until it
leaves the display; any key aborts the effect.
Example:
  conioxlib_bounce();
@wipe
Prototype:
void conioxlib_wipe(void);
Clears the screen with a diagonal wipe.
Parameters: none.
The screen is erased from one corner to the opposite
one without using global buffers; any key aborts it.
Example:
  conioxlib_wipe();
@ckey
Prototype:
unsigned int conioxlib_ckey(void);
Waits for a key and returns it.
Parameters: none.
While waiting it keeps the clock, the lock indicators
and the mouse status up to date, so the dialogs stay
alive.
Returns: the key code (ASCII or scan << 8).
Example:
  k = conioxlib_ckey();
  if (k == KBD_ESC)
  {
      /* cancelled */
  }
@getstr
Prototype:
void conioxlib_getstr(unsigned short x,
    unsigned short y, unsigned char atr,
    char *str, char len);
Reads a string from the keyboard at (x,y).
Parameters: atr is the color of the field, str
receives the text and is also the initial value shown,
and len is the maximum number of characters accepted.
BACKSPACE erases and ENTER accepts; ESC restores the
initial text.
Example:
  char name[15];
  strcpy(name, "CONXLIB");
  conioxlib_getstr(10, 5,
      CONIOXLIB_ATTR(WHITE, BLUE), name, 14);
@getpw
Prototype:
void conioxlib_getpw(unsigned short x,
    unsigned short y, unsigned char atr,
    char *pass, char len);
Reads a password without echoing it.
Parameters: atr is the color of the field, pass
receives the text and len is the maximum length.
Every typed character is shown as an asterisk.
Example:
  char pass[10];
  conioxlib_getpw(10, 5,
      CONIOXLIB_ATTR(WHITE, BLUE), pass, 9);
@grid
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_grid(
    unsigned short x1, unsigned short y1,
    unsigned char atr0, unsigned char atr1,
    const char items[][CONIOXLIB_GRID_W],
    unsigned char noptions);
Picks an option from a grid of four columns per row.
Parameters: atr0 is the normal color and atr1 the
selected one, items holds the texts and noptions their
number. The arrow keys, the mouse wheel and the left
button move the selection.
Returns: the index of the chosen item or 255 when
cancelled with ESC.
Example:
  i = conioxlib_grid(41, 6, atr0, atr1,
      items, 9);
@cputs_hot
Prototype:
void conioxlib_cputs_hot(unsigned short x,
    unsigned short y, unsigned char atr,
    unsigned char atrhot, const char *s);
Writes s at (x,y) highlighting its accelerator key.
Parameters: atr is the base color and atrhot the color
of the highlighted letter. An ampersand before a
letter marks it as the accelerator and is never drawn;
with no ampersand the first letter is the accelerator.
Example:
  conioxlib_cputs_hot(10, 5, atr, atrhot,
      "&File");
@buttons
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_buttons(
    unsigned short x1, unsigned short y1,
    unsigned char atr0, unsigned char atr1,
    unsigned char atrhot,
    const char items[][CONIOXLIB_BTN_W],
    const char desc[][CONIOXLIB_HINT_W],
    unsigned char noptions,
    unsigned char separation);
Draws a row of Turbo Vision style buttons.
Parameters: (x1,y1) is the top left corner, atr0 and
atr1 are the normal and pressed colors, atrhot the
accelerator color, items the labels, desc the status
line hints, noptions their number and separation the
gap between buttons. The chosen button flashes
inverted like the Turbo Vision press feedback.
Returns: the index of the pressed button, 254 when F1
was pressed or 255 when cancelled with ESC. Prefer
conioxlib_theme_btnattrs() for the colors.
Example:
  unsigned char b0, b1, bh;
  conioxlib_theme_btnattrs(&b0, &b1, &bh);
  i = conioxlib_buttons(28, 12, b0, b1, bh,
      btns, descs, 3, 2);
See also: @menu @option @property @theme
@menu
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_menu(
    unsigned short x1, unsigned short y1,
    unsigned char atr0, unsigned char atr1,
    unsigned char atrhot,
    const char items[][CONIOXLIB_ITEM_W],
    const char desc[][CONIOXLIB_HINT_W],
    unsigned char noptions,
    unsigned char separation,
    unsigned char sel);
Runs a vertical menu with status line hints.
Parameters: (x1,y1) is the top left corner, atr0 and
atr1 the normal and selected colors, atrhot the
accelerator color, items the entries, desc the hints,
noptions their number, separation the distance between
rows and sel the initial selection. Items starting
with '-' are non-selectable separators.
Returns: the chosen index, CONIOXLIB_MENU_ESC,
CONIOXLIB_MENU_HELP, CONIOXLIB_MENU_LEFT or
CONIOXLIB_MENU_RIGHT. A scroll bar appears when the
items do not fit. A pick also records the item cell as
the zoom origin for a later box(ZOOM).
Example:
  opt = conioxlib_menu(5, 4, th.atr0, th.atr1,
      th.atrhot, menu, desc, 28, 1, opt);
See also: @menubar @buttons @Keys
@menubar
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_menubar(
    unsigned short y, unsigned char atr0,
    unsigned char atr1, unsigned char atrhot,
    const char items[][CONIOXLIB_ITEM_W],
    const char desc[][CONIOXLIB_HINT_W],
    unsigned char noptions,
    unsigned char init);
Runs a Borland style top menu bar on row y.
Parameters: atr0 and atr1 are the normal and selected
colors, atrhot the accelerator color, items the
labels, desc the hints, noptions their number and init
the item highlighted on entry. The horizontal wheel
and the arrows move; ENTER or a click picks an item.
Returns: the chosen index, CONIOXLIB_MENU_ESC or
CONIOXLIB_MENU_HELP. A pick records the bar item cell
as the zoom origin for a later box(ZOOM).
Example:
  i = conioxlib_menubar(1, atr0, atr1, atrhot,
      bar, desc, 4, 0);
See also: @menu @buttons
@option
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_option(
    unsigned short x1, unsigned short y1,
    unsigned char atr0, unsigned char atr1,
    unsigned char atrhot,
    const char items[][CONIOXLIB_ITEM_W],
    const char desc[][CONIOXLIB_HINT_W],
    char *sel, unsigned char noptions,
    unsigned char separation,
    unsigned char enabled);
Runs a list of check boxes.
Parameters: sel holds one mark byte per item, a space
for unchecked and the character \xFE for checked;
enabled must be nonzero to allow interaction. The
space bar and the left button toggle the entries.
Returns: 255 when F1 was pressed or 0 when the dialog
is closed.
Example:
  memset(sel, ' ', 9);
  sel[0] = '\xFE';
  r = conioxlib_option(41, 6, atr0, atr1, atrhot,
      items, desc, sel, 9, 1, 1);
@property
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_property(
    unsigned short x1, unsigned short y1,
    unsigned char atr0, unsigned char atr1,
    unsigned char atrhot,
    const char items[][CONIOXLIB_ITEM_W],
    const char desc[][CONIOXLIB_HINT_W],
    char *sel, unsigned char noptions,
    unsigned char separation,
    unsigned char enabled);
Runs a radio button list: exactly one item is marked.
Parameters: identical to conioxlib_option(), but only
one entry holds the character \xFE mark at a time.
Returns: 255 when F1 was pressed or 0 on close.
Example:
  sel[demo_theme] = '\xFE';
  r = conioxlib_property(41, 6, atr0, atr1,
      atrhot, names, desc, sel, 9, 1, 1);
@background
Prototype:
void conioxlib_background(
    const unsigned char *msg);
Paints the application background with the colors of
the active theme and writes msg centered on line 25.
Parameters: msg is the message for the status area; an
empty string leaves the status area blank. It also
marks the status double buffer dirty so the next
conioxlib_status() repaints.
Example:
  conioxlib_background(
      (const unsigned char *) DEMO_TITLE);
@hello
Prototype:
void conioxlib_hello(const char *name,
    unsigned char commercial);
Saves the screen to a file and shows the about box.
Parameters: name is the file used to save the screen
that conioxlib_bye() will restore; commercial selects
between the shareware and the personal about text.
Under Turbo C it also installs the critical error
handler restored by conioxlib_bye().
Example:
  conioxlib_hello("CONXDEMO.TMP", 0);
See also: @bye @Introduction
@bye
Prototype:
void conioxlib_bye(const char *name,
    unsigned char e, unsigned char commercial);
Restores the screen saved by conioxlib_hello() and
ends the program.
Parameters: name is the same file given to hello(), e
is the exit code and commercial selects the farewell
text. It also frees the malloc'd help topic cache
before exit, on every exit path.
Example:
  conioxlib_bye("CONXDEMO.TMP", 0, 0);
See also: @hello @exittodos @savescr
@about
Prototype:
void conioxlib_about(unsigned char commercial);
Shows the credits box of the library.
Parameters: commercial selects between the shareware
and the personal text.
Example:
  conioxlib_about(0);
@compilerstring
Prototype:
const char * conioxlib_compilerstring(void);
Returns the name and version of the compiler used to
build the library, e.g. "Open Watcom C/C++ 2.0 beta".
Parameters: none.
The string is built once from the compiler macros and
shown in the splash screen and the About box.
Returns: a pointer to a static string.
Example:
  printf("Built with %s",
      conioxlib_compilerstring());
@exittodos
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_exittodos(
    const char *name);
Asks for confirmation before leaving to DOS.
Parameters: name is the help file shown by its F1
button.
Returns: 1 when the user confirms the exit and 0 when
he stays. The Shell answer runs conioxlib_shell() and
also returns 0.
Example:
  if (conioxlib_exittodos(DEMO_HELP) == 1)
  {
      exit(0);
  }
@screensaver
Prototype:
void conioxlib_screensaver(void);
Runs the block screen saver until a key or mouse event.
Parameters: none.
The screen is removed and put back block by block; any
key, click or wheel movement wakes it up.
Example:
  conioxlib_screensaver();
@shell
Prototype:
void conioxlib_shell(unsigned short x1,
    unsigned short y1, unsigned short x2,
    unsigned short y2, const char *command);
Runs a DOS shell inside a window.
Parameters: the rectangle frames the shell output and
command is the program to run; an empty command starts
the interactive interpreter named by COMSPEC. Under
Windows the shell takes the whole screen and the
rectangle is ignored. Type EXIT inside the shell to
come back; the screen is restored on return.
Example:
  conioxlib_shell(3, 2, 75, 22, "");
@calendar
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_calendar(
    unsigned short x, unsigned short y,
    unsigned char atr0, unsigned char atr1,
    unsigned char atr2,
    unsigned char *day,
    unsigned char *month,
    unsigned int *year);
Draws an interactive month calendar at (x,y).
Parameters: atr0 is the color of the normal days, atr1
the color of the selected day and atr2 the color of
the Sundays; day, month and year receive the date
picked with the cursors, PGUP/PGDW (month), HOME/END
(year) or the mouse.
Returns: 1 when ENTER picks a day and 0 when ESC
cancels.
Example:
  conioxlib_calendar(30, 5, atr0, atr1, atr2,
      &day, &month, &year);
@view
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_view(
    unsigned short x1, unsigned short y1,
    unsigned short x2, unsigned short y2,
    unsigned char atr0, unsigned char atr1,
    const char buffer[][CONIOXLIB_HELP_W],
    unsigned char last, const char *title);
Displays a scrollable text viewer.
Parameters: buffer holds the lines (60 usable
columns), last is the number of lines stored, atr0
the text color, atr1 the selected line color and title
the frame title. The wheel, the arrows, PGUP, PGDN,
HOME and END move; clicking a line jumps to it.
Returns: 255 when cancelled with ESC.
Example:
  r = conioxlib_view(6, 3, 72, 21, atr0, atr1,
      helpbuf, n - 1, "Help: getkey");
See also: @help @helpindex
@help
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_help(
    unsigned short x1, unsigned short y1,
    unsigned short x2, unsigned short y2,
    unsigned char atr0, unsigned char atr1,
    const char *name, const char *topic,
    char buffer[][CONIOXLIB_HELP_W]);
Shows one topic of a help file.
Parameters: name is the help file, topic the topic to
find (its header line starts with @) and buffer
area for the text. The topic table is malloc'd on the
first call and reused afterwards; conioxlib_bye()
frees it.
Returns: 1 when the topic was found and 0 when it was
not or the file is missing. Lines are cut at 60
characters and the next @ line ends the entry.
Example:
  r = conioxlib_help(7, 3, 69, 21, atr0, atr1,
      DEMO_HELP, "getkey", helpbuf);
See also: @helpindex @view @Introduction
@helpindex
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_helpindex(
    unsigned short x1, unsigned short y1,
    unsigned char atr0, unsigned char atr1,
    const char *name,
    char buffer[][16]);
Lists every topic of a help file and opens the picked
one.
Parameters: name is the help file and buffer a work
area that receives the topic names (truncated at 15
characters). The list comes from the malloc'd topic
cache, so only the first call scans the file.
Returns: the index of the topic shown or 255 when
cancelled.
Example:
  conioxlib_helpindex(5, 3, atr0, atr1,
      DEMO_HELP, helpbuf);
See also: @help @Index
@filemultisel
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_filemultiselector(
    unsigned char *sdisk, char *sdir,
    struct conioxlib_sfiles *files);
Runs the file selector with multiple marks.
Parameters: sdisk receives the chosen drive letter,
sdir the chosen directory and files the table with the
name, size, date, time, description and selected mark
of every entry. F2 changes the mask, INS or SPACE mark
a file, F3 marks all, F4 unmarks all and ENTER enters
a directory or accepts a drive.
Returns: 0 when accepted and 255 when cancelled.
Example:
  disk = 'C';
  if (conioxlib_filemultiselector(&disk,
      dir, files) == 0)
  {
      sprintf(str, "%c:%s", disk, dir);
  }
See also: @fileselector @listbox
@fileselector
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_fileselector(
    unsigned char *sdisk, char *sdir,
    char *name);
Runs a compact picker for a single file: drives,
directories and names only, without sizes, dates or
descriptions, so it fits the Open and Save as dialogs
of the editor.
Parameters: sdisk receives the chosen drive letter,
sdir the chosen directory and name the chosen file
name. The list shows long names when the system has
them but the name returned is always the short 8.3
one.
Returns: 1 when a file was chosen and 0 when
cancelled.
Example:
  if (conioxlib_fileselector(&disk, dir,
      name) != 0)
  {
      sprintf(str, "%c:%s%s", disk, dir, name);
  }
See also: @filemultisel @listbox
@theme
Prototype:
void conioxlib_theme(unsigned char id);
Applies one of the nine visual themes.
Parameters: id is 0 Borland C++ 3.1, 1 Turbo C 2.0, 2
Turbo C 1.x, 3 Norton Utilities 9, 4 PC Tools, 5 Turbo
Pascal 7.0, 6 Norton Commander, 7 MS-DOS Edit and 8
Lotus 1-2-3. Themes 0 and 5 share the real Turbo
Vision 2.0 palette used by both Borland IDEs. Each
theme also owns its button colors; see the
theme_btnattrs topic.
Example:
  conioxlib_theme(1);
See also: @gettheme @theme_btnattrs @Colors
@gettheme
Prototype:
void conioxlib_gettheme(
    struct conioxlib_stheme *t);
Copies the active theme into t.
Parameters: t receives atr0, atr1, atrhot, atrstatus,
atrdialog, the per-theme button colors btnatr0,
btnatr1, btnatrhot, the frame string and the theme
name.
Example:
  struct conioxlib_stheme th;
  conioxlib_gettheme(&th);
  box(3, 3, 36, 23, th.atrdialog, th.frame,
      "CONXLIB 2.0", SHADOW | CLOSE);
See also: @theme @theme_btnattrs
@theme_btnattrs
Prototype:
void conioxlib_theme_btnattrs(
    unsigned char *atr0,
    unsigned char *atr1,
    unsigned char *atrhot);
Fills atr0/atr1/atrhot with the button colors of the
active theme.
Parameters: atr0 receives the button face color, atr1
the selected button color and atrhot the accelerator
color. Each theme owns its triple: a Norton Commander
dialog paints cyan buttons, a Lotus dialog
black-on-white ones, and so on, instead of the fixed
Turbo Vision green.
Example:
  unsigned char b0, b1, bh;
  conioxlib_theme_btnattrs(&b0, &b1, &bh);
  conioxlib_buttons(20, 12, b0, b1, bh,
      btns, descs, 2, 2);
See also: @theme @gettheme @buttons
@savescr
Prototype:
char *conioxlib_savescr(unsigned short x1,
    unsigned short y1, unsigned short x2,
    unsigned short y2);
Saves a screen region with malloc.
Parameters: the rectangle to save.
Returns: the buffer or 0 when there is no memory. Free
it with conioxlib_restorescr().
Example:
  oldb = conioxlib_savescr(3, 2, 75, 22);
@restorescr
Prototype:
void conioxlib_restorescr(
    unsigned short x1, unsigned short y1,
    unsigned short x2, unsigned short y2,
    char *buf);
Restores a region saved by conioxlib_savescr() and
frees the buffer.
Parameters: the same rectangle given on save and the
buffer returned by conioxlib_savescr(). When the
region overlaps the status strip it also marks the
status double buffer dirty.
Example:
  conioxlib_restorescr(3, 2, 75, 22, oldb);
@status
Prototype:
void conioxlib_status(const char *msg);
Writes msg on the status line.
Parameters: msg is the text to show.
The status line lives in the status strip of the last
row; the lock indicators and the clock share the rest
of the line. A double buffer holds the last painted
text: when msg is unchanged and nothing has wiped the
strip, the puttext is skipped, so the per-frame hint
refresh of the menu loop does not repaint line 25.
Example:
  conioxlib_status("Ready.");
@tooltip
Prototype:
void conioxlib_tooltip(unsigned short x,
    unsigned short y, const char *msg,
    unsigned int ms);
Shows a tooltip near (x,y) during ms milliseconds.
Parameters: msg is the text of the tip.
Example:
  conioxlib_tooltip(50, 12,
      "Tooltip sample", 1500);
@progressbar
Prototype:
void conioxlib_progressbar(
    unsigned short x, unsigned short y,
    unsigned short width, unsigned char atr,
    unsigned int value, unsigned int maxv);
Draws an installer style progress ruler.
Parameters: (x,y) is the position, width the length in
cells, atr the color, and value and maxv the
proportion shown: the ruler spans width - 5 cells as a
0xB0 grid that fills up with solid 0xDB cells like the
Turbo Vision gauges and the percentage number follows
it on the right.
Example:
  conioxlib_progressbar(22, 13, 30, atr,
      done, total);
@progress
Prototype:
void conioxlib_progress(const char *title,
    unsigned int value, unsigned int maxv);
Shows an installer style gauge on the status line.
Parameters: title is the label drawn at column 1 and
value and maxv the proportion. The gauge starts after
the label with the status line color of the active
theme and conioxlib_progressbar() ends it with the
percentage number on the right.
Example:
  for (i = 0; i <= 100; i++)
  {
      conioxlib_progress("Copying", i, 100);
      delay(50);
  }
@msgbox
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_msgbox(
    const char *title, const char *msg,
    char buttons[][CONIOXLIB_BTN_W],
    unsigned char nbuttons);
Shows a message box with up to three buttons.
Parameters: title is the caption, msg the text, buttons
the labels and nbuttons their number (1 to 3); passing
0 as buttons uses the defaults OK and Cancel.
Returns: as conioxlib_buttons() does: the index of the
pressed button, 254 when F1 was pressed or 255 when
cancelled.
Example:
  r = conioxlib_msgbox("Confirm",
      "Exit the demo?", btns, 2);
@inputbox
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_inputbox(
    const char *title, const char *label,
    char *buf, char len);
Asks for a string in a dialog box.
Parameters: title is the caption, label the text next
to the field, buf receives the answer and is also the
initial value, and len the maximum length.
Returns: 0 when accepted with ENTER, 1 when cancelled
and 255 when F1 was pressed.
Example:
  r = conioxlib_inputbox("Open", "File:",
      name, 60);
@inputmask
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_inputmask(
    const char *title, const char *label,
    char *buf, char len, char mindigits);
Asks for a digits-only string in a dialog box.
Parameters: like conioxlib_inputbox(), plus mindigits:
the OK button only accepts the answer when at least
that many digits were typed. Invalid characters are
ignored as they are pressed.
Returns: 0 when accepted, 1 when cancelled and 255 when
F1 was pressed.
Example:
  r = conioxlib_inputmask("Port", "Value:",
      buf, 5, 2);
@listbox
Prototype:
unsigned char conioxlib_listbox(
    unsigned short x1, unsigned short y1,
    unsigned short x2, unsigned short y2,
    unsigned char atr0, unsigned char atr1,
    const char items[][CONIOXLIB_LISTITEM_W],
    unsigned char nitems,
    const char *title);
Runs a scrollable list box with a scroll bar.
Parameters: atr0 is the normal color, atr1 the selected
one, items the texts (up to 40 characters), nitems
their number and title the caption. The arrows, PGUP,
PGDN, HOME, END and the wheel move; ENTER or a click
chooses a row and a click on the bar jumps to that
position. Typing a letter or an Alt+letter jumps to
the item whose accelerator matches (it reuses the menu
hot-key matcher); ENTER still picks.
Returns: the index of the chosen item or 255 when
cancelled with ESC.
Example:
  i = conioxlib_listbox(30, 6, 55, 18, atr0,
      atr1, items, 26, "Pick one");
See also: @grid @fileselector @filemultisel
@error
Prototype (Turbo C DOS builds only):
void interrupt error(unsigned int, ...);
Critical error handler installed by conioxlib_hello().
Parameters: the interrupt frame registers.
It keeps the CRITICAL ERROR flag set for
conioxlib_shell(). Do not call it directly;
conioxlib_bye() restores the previous handler.
Example:
  /* installed automatically by
     conioxlib_hello() */
@shell_patch
Prototype (Turbo C DOS builds only):
void interrupt conioxlib_shell_patch(
    unsigned int, ...);
Int 29h filter installed by conioxlib_shell() under
Turbo C.
Parameters: the interrupt frame registers.
It redirects the CON output of the child process into
the shell window. Do not call it directly.
Example:
  /* installed automatically by
     conioxlib_shell() */

